\author{Compiled by Claude Sonnet 5.5 (claude-sonnet-5-5) for Andr\'as Kornai}
\date{}
\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Sharwa (ISO 639-3: swq; Glottocode shar1249) is a Central Chadic (Afro-Asiatic) language of northern Cameroon. Alternative names are Tchevi, Sherwin and Sarwaye. About 5,100 speakers were counted in 2000. Sources note a shift towards Fulfulde. Wikipedia describes it as closely related to Jimi.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
The searches found no primers, textbooks or school materials for Sharwa.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item SIL Cameroon: a rapid appraisal language survey of Sharwa (Mayo-Tsanaga Division, Far North Province). It assessed whether a written form should be developed: \url{https://www.silcam.org/resources/archives/9208}
\item Abstract by Richard Gravina (WOCAL) comparing Sharwa and Jimi (vowels, consonants, prosody): \url{https://www.uni-koeln.de/phil-fak/afrikanistik/wocal/schedule/abstracts/4-6-2%20Richard%20Gravina.pdf}
\item Wikipedia entry for Sharwa: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sharwa_language}
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Joshua Project profile, Sharwa in Cameroon. It reports that Scripture translation has begun and that the community is mostly Muslim: \url{https://joshuaproject.net/people_groups/19046/CM}
\end{itemize}
A Joshua Project page for ``Sarwa'' in Chad also appeared. I could not confirm it is the same group, so I do not list it.

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
The searches found no audio, video or software resources for Sharwa.

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
Very little exists. No orthography or school materials were found. The survey and the Sharwa--Jimi phonology work are the starting points for developing a writing system. Fulfulde pressure suggests the community would need to be consulted on any school use.
\end{document}
